@@ -314,3 +314,4 @@ Appended after the final whole-branch review of Milestone 1; the approved text a
 - **Doctor port findings are re-evaluated after allocation (cycle 4, Codex connector review of `83b468e71`).** The doctor preflight inspects the envrc ports before `_resolve_ports` may lease replacements, so for `start`/`restart` its port-dependent findings (`PORT_RESERVED_COLLISION`, `PORT_INTRA_CONFIG_COLLISION`, `PORT_OWNED_BY_OTHER_PROJECT`, `DOCKER_CONTAINER_WRONG_PROJECT`, `DOCKER_CONTAINER_UNLABELED_UNKNOWN`, `DOCKER_CONTAINER_PORT_COLLISION`) are no longer carried as blockers; they are recorded as `DOCTOR_PORT_FINDING_REEVALUATED` INFO entries in the preflight facts (visible on blocked results; a successful start's output lists no preflight warnings, which is pre-existing `_start_services` behaviour) and the lifecycle's own collision checks against the resolved ports (plus `PORT_ALLOCATION_BLOCKED` when no free port exists) decide. After `init` → `stop` a foreign container on the stale envrc port no longer blocks a start that re-leased a free port. `stop`/`status` keep the doctor findings as before. The lifecycle's collision check now applies the launcher's ownership rule to any managed worktree-scoped container holding a resolved port: a same-project container of another worktree (different `dopemux.worktree_hash`), or a managed container without a project id whose project root does not match, is a `DOCKER_CONTAINER_PORT_COLLISION`; containers dopemux itself labels are therefore re-checked against the resolved ports, while a managed container without dopemux's project labels (accepted on compose evidence) is not, and only `dopemux mcp doctor` reports those. A project-scoped task-orchestrator container started from another worktree of the same project is no longer blocked by the doctor's workspace mismatch (the orchestrator gate under the reserved lock decides).
 - **Cycle 5 narrows the Task F re-evaluation to one invariant (Codex audit at `fc591cba1`).** For `start`/`restart` every doctor blocker is carried except a `DOCKER_CONTAINER_*` finding whose container neither publishes a port in the resolved port map nor bears one of this run's expected container names (that one becomes a `DOCTOR_PORT_FINDING_REEVALUATED` INFO entry), and the resolved port map is validated: two selected services on one port or a sidecar on a reserved singleton port block as `PORT_INTRA_CONFIG_COLLISION` / `PORT_RESERVED_COLLISION` even when `_resolve_ports` retained existing leases. The `PORT_*` doctor codes are no longer dropped. Consequently the two behaviour changes recorded in the previous bullet — a managed container with only compose labels holding our expected name, and a project-scoped task-orchestrator container started from another worktree of the same project — block again exactly as on main; the stale-envrc-port recovery (the connector finding) is kept.
 - **Cycle 5 revert: the automatic-reap change for marker-only runtime dirs on an empty registry (`380c2c52c`) was reverted (`2bbf08f65`).** The independent audit found that it covered an empty registry file but not an absent one (on a fresh host `RuntimeRegistry.load()` reports MISSING and `plan_reap` plans nothing unless the registry is OK), so the Codex PR bot's scenario — launcher-marked runtime dirs of removed worktrees left by build-only launches never reaching the automatic reap while the registry lists no instance — stayed open, and that marker discovery ran unbounded by the automatic reap's deadline or a count cap. Rather than a fourth change to the reap preflight in this PR, the owner chose the revert; the finding is an open follow-up outside this packet. The explicit `dopemux mcp reap` reaches those dirs only once the runtime registry file exists (a non-dry-run `start`, including a launcher's cold start, creates it); on a host where only build-only launches ran there is no such file, so it plans nothing either, as the how-to's Cleanup paragraph says ("no run sees it while that registry file is missing or unreadable").
+- **Cycle 7 open follow-up: `start --dry-run` and `restart --dry-run` persist port leases (Codex connector review of `f6a8e4401`, `lifecycle.py:1081`).** `_mutable_facts` resolves ports with `allocate=True` for `start` and `restart` whether or not the run is a dry run, and `_resolve_ports` then releases conflicting leases, saves the lease registry and calls the allocator with persistence on. A plan-only run therefore creates the lease registry file if it is absent and leaves active leases for the selected lease-managed services, and it can replace an envrc port that another worktree has leased. The behaviour predates the merge of main (the code is unchanged since `40ea89c24`), and five tests in `tests/unit/test_mcp_lifecycle_ports_from_leases.py` exercise allocation through dry runs and assert on the persisted leases, so changing it means threading the dry-run flag into port resolution, computing a non-persisting preview and rewriting those tests. The owner chose not to make that change in this PR; it is an open follow-up outside this packet. Until then a dry run reserves the ports the real start would take; what later releases a lease that only a dry run created was not examined.
